<aside>
  <h4 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2">Codec Preference</h4>

  <section class="ml-2 md:ml-4 mb-2 max-w-prose text-sm leading-5 text-theme-on-surface-muted">
    <p>
      Resolution is chosen first. At the same resolution, codecs are tried in the order you
      list them, so <code class="text-sm">VP9, AVC, AV1</code> prefers VP9 and falls back to AVC
      and then AV1. Audio is ordered independently, after your audio-track and language choice.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-4">
      NOTE: this is a <em>soft</em>
      preference. If none of your listed codecs is available, <code class="text-sm">yt-dlp</code>
      downloads its next best format. A preference is not an exclusion rule. An empty list means
      no application codec preference for that stream, so yt-dlp's defaults or your custom
      <code class="text-sm">-S</code>
      sorting apply. <.inline_link href="https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp#sorting-formats">See here</.inline_link>
      for more information and a list of available codecs.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-4">
      Supported names match yt-dlp's codec families, for example <code class="text-sm">avc</code>/<code class="text-sm">avc1</code>/<code class="text-sm">h264</code>, <code class="text-sm">vp9</code>/<code class="text-sm">vp09</code>, <code class="text-sm">av1</code>/<code class="text-sm">av01</code>
      for video and <code class="text-sm">m4a</code>
      (AAC in MP4, including <code class="text-sm">aac</code>
      and <code class="text-sm">mp4a</code>), <code class="text-sm">opus</code>, <code class="text-sm">vorbis</code>, <code class="text-sm">flac</code>, <code class="text-sm">wav</code>/<code class="text-sm">aiff</code>
      for audio. Aliases are saved as their canonical names (for example, <code class="text-sm">H264</code>
      becomes <code class="text-sm">avc</code>, <code class="text-sm">aac</code>
      becomes <code class="text-sm">m4a</code>, and <code class="text-sm">aiff</code>
      becomes <code class="text-sm">wav</code>). A list may
      hold up to 8 codecs.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-4">
      A preferred audio codec is the codec of the source audio track. It is not the final
      container, which still follows your profile's audio format and remux settings.
    </p>
  </section>
</aside>
